// Box2D's own structs, named from the layer an application imports.
//
// **A transparent alias is a second spelling, not a second type.** `type Polygon = c.Polygon`
// declares nothing of its own: a value built through either name is the same value, crosses to C in
// the same registers, and satisfies both names everywhere one of them is written. This file costs
// nothing at run time.
//
// **Why an alias rather than a move.** The declarations below are what `c`'s own signatures take —
// `b2CreatePolygonShape` is written against a `*Polygon` — and a module may not depend on a module
// that depends on it, so they cannot be lifted the way a `c const` block can. Naming them from above
// is the other half of the same idea, and needs sysl 0.0.67.
//
// **What is here is what a public signature of this module names**, which is the test the split
// actually asks: a program writing a ray-cast callback has to spell `ShapeId`, and one reading a
// contact has to spell `ContactBeginTouchEvent`. Everything else in `c` stays there, because nothing
// above ever hands one to a caller.
//
// **Two names are missing and both are taken.** `ContactEvents` and `SensorEvents` are declared in
// `box2d.sysl` as this layer's own wrappers, holding slices of the raw events below; aliasing the
// raw ones to those names would hide the wrappers. Their element types are here, which is what a
// program iterating them needs.
//
// **Five of these names are also variants of `ShapeKind` or `JointKind`** — `Circle`, `Capsule`,
// `Segment`, `Polygon` and `Filter` — and in *call* position the variant wins, so `Circle(c, r)`
// is read as the variant and refused for taking arguments. In *type* position, which is what these
// names are for, there is no such contest and the alias resolves. Build one with `circle`,
// `capsule`, `segment`, `filter` or `make_box` in `defs.sysl` rather than by naming the struct;
// those are the pleasant spellings anyway, and they are what every example uses.

module sh.sysl.box2d

import sh.sysl.box2d.c

// ## The handles
//
// Each is an index and a generation rather than a pointer, which is what makes a stale one
// detectable rather than a use-after-free. `World`, `Body`, `Shape`, `Chain` and `Joint` wrap them,
// but a callback handed to Box2D is called with the raw id and has to be able to say so.

type WorldId = c.WorldId
type BodyId = c.BodyId
type ShapeId = c.ShapeId
type ChainId = c.ChainId
type JointId = c.JointId

// ## The shapes
//
// A polygon is a fixed 144 bytes whatever its vertex count, which is why all four are passed and
// returned by value everywhere: Box2D never allocates one.

type Circle = c.Circle
type Capsule = c.Capsule
type Segment = c.Segment
type Polygon = c.Polygon

// ## What describes a thing before it exists
//
// `body_def`, `shape_def` and `filter` in `defs.sysl` build these with every field defaulted to
// Box2D's own default, so a program names the type only when it wants to hold one.

type BodyDef = c.BodyDef
type ShapeDef = c.ShapeDef
type Filter = c.Filter
type QueryFilter = c.QueryFilter
type SurfaceMaterial = c.SurfaceMaterial

// ## What comes back

type MassData = c.MassData
type RayCastInput = c.RayCastInput
type CastOutput = c.CastOutput
type Manifold = c.Manifold
type ManifoldPoint = c.ManifoldPoint

// ## The events a step leaves behind
//
// Each is a view into the world's own storage and is valid until the next `step`.

type BodyMoveEvent = c.BodyMoveEvent
type ContactBeginTouchEvent = c.ContactBeginTouchEvent
type ContactEndTouchEvent = c.ContactEndTouchEvent
type ContactHitEvent = c.ContactHitEvent
type SensorBeginTouchEvent = c.SensorBeginTouchEvent
type SensorEndTouchEvent = c.SensorEndTouchEvent
